\section{Why reconcile rather than replace}{

A lookup cannot serve as a counting dictionary. Its \code{Code} field holds Tilia's
display abbreviation, not the one- or two-character keystroke code you type at
the microscope, and it lists tens of thousands of taxa where a working
dictionary holds tens. The lookup is an authority to check against; your
dictionary stays yours.

The payoff is chiefly Neotoma submission via \code{\link[=write_tlx]{write_tlx()}}: names that match
Neotoma's authority list need no reconciliation on upload. It also surfaces
deprecated names and misfiled groups you may want to correct regardless.
}

\section{The status column}{

Each row is classified by how its name matched, in this order:
\describe{
\item{\code{exact}}{Name found verbatim among accepted taxa. Nothing to do.}
\item{\code{alias}}{Matched an entry in your own \code{aliases} file. Your
assertion, so safe to apply in bulk.}
\item{\code{variant}}{Differs from an accepted name only in orthography --
diacritics, \code{c.f.}/\code{cf.}, \code{subgen.}/\code{subg.}. Safe to apply
mechanically.}
\item{\code{synonym}}{Neotoma's own synonymy maps the name to a different
accepted taxon. Authoritative, but a taxonomic judgement: review it,
and note the revision can run either way. Modern segregates such as
\emph{Spinulum annotinum} may be \emph{newer} than the lookup's
\emph{Lycopodium annotinum}.}
\item{\code{suggestion}}{No authoritative match; the closest name by string
similarity is shown with its score. \strong{Never applied as a class} --
only ever by code or name. String similarity is unreliable on taxon
names, and no cutoff makes it reliable: \verb{Cerealia undiff.} scores 0.75
against \emph{Sordaria} undiff., a fungus, while the correct
\verb{Dendrolycopodium obscurum} -> \emph{Lycopodium obscurum} scores only 0.72.
Scores also tie, in which case the candidate shown is whichever comes
first in the pool rather than the best answer.}
\item{\code{unmatched}}{No match and no candidate above \code{cutoff}.}
}
